<div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><section class="snap"><h2>Lexington at a glance</h2>
<dl class="snap-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Fayette County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>The Bluegrass</dd></div><div><dt>Housing mix</dt><dd>Historic brick homes near downtown and in older districts give way to mid-century ranches and large planned subdivisions, with horse farms and rural estates ringing the urban service boundary.</dd></div></dl>
<h3>Local claim risks</h3><ul class="tags"><li>Ice storms and limb falls</li><li>Hail</li><li>Straight-line wind</li><li>Barn and outbuilding damage</li><li>Frozen pipes</li></ul>
<h3>Neighborhoods and nearby communities</h3><ul class="tags light"><li>Chevy Chase</li><li>Ashland Park</li><li>Hamburg</li><li>Masterson Station</li><li>Beaumont</li><li>Gardenside</li><li>Kenwick</li><li>Bell Court</li><li>Southland</li><li>Tates Creek</li></ul>
<h3>Weather history</h3><ol class="mini-tl"><li><b>1974</b><span>The April 3 Super Outbreak produced tornadoes across central Kentucky, including the Bluegrass region.</span></li><li><b>2003</b><span>A February ice storm coated Lexington’s trees and lines, bringing down limbs across much of the city.</span></li><li><b>2008</b><span>Remnants of Hurricane Ike swept a damaging windstorm through central Kentucky in September.</span></li><li><b>2023</b><span>The March 3 high-wind event downed trees and damaged roofs across the Bluegrass.</span></li></ol></section>
<h2>Trees, Ice and Roofs in a Mature City</h2>
<p>Lexington has a lot of mature canopy, and that shapes its claims. When ice or high wind arrives, limbs come down on roofs, gutters, decks and fences. The 2003 ice storm is still the reference point for many owners. A tree claim tends to involve several moving parts at once: removal, roofing, framing, interior water and sometimes a fence or detached garage, each with its own limits.</p>
<p>Disputes often turn on <strong>scope</strong>. Did the impact crack rafters or only shingles? Is the remaining roof repairable, or does a patch leave a mismatched, failing system? Our <a href="/appraisal-calculators/tree-removal-coverage-calculator/">tree removal coverage calculator</a> can help sort out what portion of debris work is likely covered before you argue the bigger numbers.</p>
<h2>Where Bluegrass Estimates Diverge</h2>
<p>Inside New Circle Road, homes in Chevy Chase, Ashland Park and Bell Court often have plaster, hardwood floors and original windows. Out in Hamburg and Masterson Station, the houses are newer but the subdivisions use specific siding colors and shingle lines that are sometimes discontinued within a decade. Common gaps we see:</p>
<ul>
<li>Pricing a single slope when a discontinued shingle cannot be matched.</li>
<li>Omitting steep-pitch or high-roof charges on two-story homes.</li>
<li>Leaving out contractor overhead and profit on jobs that clearly need several trades.</li>
<li>Drying and reconstruction priced at averages that do not reflect local labor.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Farm and Equine Property Around the Edges</h2>
<p>Fayette County is still farm country past the suburbs. Barns, run-in sheds, board fencing and arenas are expensive to rebuild, and they rarely fit a residential estimating template. Timber framing, specialty doors and long fence runs need careful measurement. If your loss involves agricultural structures, our <a href="/insurance-appraisal/farm-equine-property-appraisal/">farm and equine property appraisal</a> page explains how we handle scheduled farm coverage and outbuilding limits.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Timing matters</b>Many policies require a sworn proof of loss or set a deadline for suit. Read your policy conditions early so the appraisal does not run past a date that matters.</div>
<h2>Working With Fayette County Owners</h2>
<p>We inspect on site throughout Lexington and the surrounding Bluegrass counties. You get an itemized estimate, photos and measurements, and a clear explanation of the gap between your number and the carrier’s. If both appraisers cannot agree, the dispute goes to an umpire, and only items in disagreement are submitted. Most of the time, a well-documented estimate narrows the difference before it gets that far.</p>
<p>Owners in surrounding counties such as Scott, Woodford and Jessamine bring us the same kinds of disputes, and the approach does not change: measure carefully, price with current local costs, and explain each difference in plain terms so that the other appraiser, and if needed the umpire, can follow the reasoning line by line.</p>
<h2>Appraisal services available in Lexington</h2><p>Every Kentucky Insurance Appraiser service is available to owners in Lexington and across Fayette County, including:</p><ul><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/party-appraiser-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/home-insurance-appraisal/">Homeowners Insurance Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/roof-hail-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/tornado-wind-appraisal/">Tornado &amp; Wind Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/fire-smoke-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Lexington claim questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Does appraisal work for barns and fences in Fayette County?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>It can, as long as the structure is covered and the dispute is about the amount of loss. Farm policies often have separate limits for outbuildings and fencing, so we review those first.</p></details><details><summary>My Lexington roof was patched after a storm. Can I still dispute it?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Possibly. If the patch left a visible mismatch or the original damage was broader than the repair, documentation of the remaining roof can support a larger claim.</p></details><details><summary>Do you serve areas outside Lexington?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Yes. We cover all 120 Kentucky counties and regularly work in Scott, Woodford, Jessamine, Clark and Bourbon counties around Lexington.</p></details></div>
